  "name": "WESTERN BLOT TEST FOR HIV.",
  "code": "WBHIV",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Result</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Criteria / Findings</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Positive</strong></td>\n<td>Presence of antibodies to <strong>at least two of the following HIV proteins</strong>: gp120/gp160, gp41, p24 (bands seen on blot)</td>\n<td>Confirmed HIV infection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Negative</strong></td>\n<td>No HIV-specific bands detected</td>\n<td>No evidence of HIV infection (if test done after window period)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Indeterminate</strong></td>\n<td>Some bands present but do not meet criteria for positivity (e.g., single band p24 or gp41 only)</td>\n<td>Inconclusive &ndash; may represent early seroconversion, cross-reactivity, or non-specific binding. Repeat testing recommended after 2&ndash;4 weeks and/or confirm with other methods (HIV RNA PCR, p24 antigen).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"846\" data-end=\"948\">\n<p data-start=\"848\" data-end=\"948\">Western Blot was historically the <strong data-start=\"882\" data-end=\"903\">confirmatory test</strong> for HIV after a positive ELISA/rapid test.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"949\" data-end=\"1038\">\n<p data-start=\"951\" data-end=\"1038\">A <strong data-start=\"953\" data-end=\"972\">positive result</strong> requires reactivity against <strong data-start=\"1001\" data-end=\"1035\">multiple specific HIV antigens</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1039\" data-end=\"1128\">\n<p data-start=\"1041\" data-end=\"1128\"><strong data-start=\"1041\" data-end=\"1066\">Indeterminate results</strong> require follow-up with repeat testing or molecular methods.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1129\" data-end=\"1326\">\n<p data-start=\"1131\" data-end=\"1326\">Now, WHO and CDC guidelines recommend <strong data-start=\"1169\" data-end=\"1243\">4th-generation HIV Ag/Ab assays + HIV-1/2 differentiation immunoassays</strong> instead of Western blot for confirmation, but some labs still report WB results.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>",
